.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_READ 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_read \- read body bytes as they arrive (streaming handlers)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ssize_t chttpsvr_req_read(chttpsvr_req *req, void *buf, size_t buflen);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Reads up to buflen bytes of the request body into buf, batch by batch,
as they arrive on the connection.  This call reads the socket itself.
The streaming thread that runs the handler does that read, and the
reactor thread never does it.  The call blocks that thread until one
of these happens: at least one byte is available, the body ends, an
error happens, chttpsvr_config_t.stream_read_timeout_us expires with
no new data, chttpsvr_config_t.max_body_read_duration_us expires when
you set that field, or the average rate of the body since the first
call falls below chttpsvr_config_t.min_transfer_rate_bps once
chttpsvr_config_t.min_transfer_rate_grace_us has passed.

Return values:
  > 0   The number of bytes that the function copied into buf.
    0   The function reached the end of the body, or buflen is 0.  A
        buflen of 0 is valid, and the call then does nothing.
   -1   One of six things happened.  req is NULL.  buf is NULL while
        buflen is above 0.  chttpsvr_register_handler registered the
        handler as a buffered handler, instead of
        chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler.  The connection dropped,
        or something reset it.  stream_read_timeout_us or
        max_body_read_duration_us expired, or the rate floor ended
        the read.  Or the body went above
        max_body_size in mid-stream.  Call chttpsvr_req_stream_error
        immediately after this call to tell the last three apart.
        Those three are real transfer errors that depend on the data.
        That function reports ccol_success for the case where buf is
        NULL and for the case of the wrong handler type, and you
        cannot tell that apart from "no error".  Both of those are
        static programming errors.  They happen on every call to the
        code at fault, and not only sometimes.

The function returns 0 at once when buflen is 0, whatever buf holds.
A buf of NULL with a buflen of 0 is therefore valid, and the call
returns 0 and does nothing.  This matches the rules of POSIX read(2).
buf must not be NULL only when buflen is above 0.

A call returns the body bytes that the connection has on hand, up to
buflen, and waits only when it has none.  It reads the socket at most
once for that, and one read brings at most 8 KiB of body, so a call
can return far fewer bytes than buflen while more of the body is still
on its way.  As with read(2), only a return of 0 means that the body
ended.

This function exists for a streaming handler that
chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler registered.  Use
chttpsvr_req_body for a buffered handler.

Two conditions together make the first call to this function for a
request write the interim "100 Continue" response.  The first is that
the request carried "Expect: 100-continue".  The second is that the
request really has a body, that is, it has a Content-Length or a
chunked Transfer-Encoding.  That interim response goes out before the
function tries to read anything.  It tells the client to go ahead and
upload the body.  A streaming handler can instead reject a request
outright, for example for bad authentication or an unacceptable
Content-Type.  Such a handler writes a final response and never calls
this function.  The server then skips that interim response
completely.  The client sees the real rejection directly, exactly as
it does when it never sends "Expect: 100-continue".  The server never
asks it to upload a body that the server was not going to read.  A
request that carries "Expect: 100-continue" but has no body at all
also gets no interim response, for the same reason: there is nothing
to invite the client to upload.

The library parses and checks the trailer fields of a chunked request
body (RFC 7230 4.1.2) as a part of the arrival at the end of the body.
It then discards them.  They are not request headers, and
chttpsvr_req_header never gives one back.  See that page for the
reason.
.fi
